---
// Obelisk licence terms: what a licence lets you DO with the software. Buying, payment,
// withdrawal, warranty and liability are deliberately NOT here — they belong to the shop's
// terms of sale / withdrawal information (German consumer law voids home-made clauses on
// those). A licence keeps the terms in force on the day it was bought: a change to the
// Personal plan's terms (plans.ts) = a new version below, never an edit of version 1.
import LegalPage from '../components/LegalPage.astro';
import { LEGAL, SITE } from '../site';
const L = LEGAL;
const SHOP = 'https://northharbourinstruments.com/policies';
---
<LegalPage title="Licence terms" heading="Obelisk licence terms" updated="2026-11-10">
  <p>
    These terms set out what you may do with Obelisk, the film negative conversion software
    from {L.trading} ({L.name}). They apply to the free trial and to Personal licences.
    Professional and Lab licences are agreed with each customer; where that agreement says
    nothing different, these terms apply to them too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Buying a licence, payment, your right of withdrawal, warranty and liability are covered by
    the <a href={`${SHOP}/terms-of-service`}>terms</a> and
    {' '}<a href={`${SHOP}/refund-policy`}>withdrawal and refund information</a> of our shop,
    and by the law.
  </p>
  <p class="muted small">Version 1, in force from 10 November 2026.</p>

  <h2>1. The free trial</h2>
  <p>
    Obelisk runs free for {SITE.trialDays} days from its first launch on a computer, with every
    feature except export: saving TIFF and JPEG files, contact sheets and profile exports. The
    trial is for trying Obelisk out. When it ends, Obelisk stops working until a licence is
    installed. Nothing you did in the trial is deleted.
  </p>

  <h2>2. The Personal licence</h2>
  <p>
    A Personal licence comes as a licence file issued in your name. It gives you a
    non-exclusive right to use Obelisk, with no time limit:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For one person:</strong> you, the person named in the licence file.</li>
    <li><strong>On up to two computers</strong> that you use.</li>
    <li><strong>With every feature</strong> of Obelisk.</li>
    <li><strong>Up to 500 exported frames per calendar month</strong> (section 3).</li>
    <li>
      <strong>With one year of updates:</strong> every version of Obelisk released up to the
      “updates until” date in your licence file, one year after your purchase. Your licence
      keeps working with those versions for good. Versions released after that date need a
      renewed licence.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. The monthly export allowance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      An exported frame is a frame you save as a TIFF or JPEG image. Each frame counts once per
      calendar month: exporting it again, or as both TIFF and JPEG, does not count again.
    </li>
    <li>Contact sheets and profile exports do not count.</li>
    <li>
      An export that is running when you reach your allowance finishes. Further exports wait
      for the next calendar month, or for a licence with a larger allowance.
    </li>
    <li>Everything else, such as opening, grading and calibrating, keeps working at the limit.</li>
    <li>Unused frames do not carry over to the next month.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Your licence file</h2>
  <p>
    Your licence file contains your name, email address, order number and the terms of your
    licence, signed so that they cannot be changed. Obelisk shows them under Help → Licence.
    Keep the file to yourself. If you lose it, we will send it to you again, free of charge.
  </p>

  <h2>5. What is not allowed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Using Obelisk beyond what your licence allows: on more computers, by more people, or
      past your monthly allowance.
    </li>
    <li>Sharing or publishing your licence file.</li>
    <li>Renting out or lending Obelisk, or letting other people use it under your licence.</li>
    <li>Removing, bypassing or tampering with the licence check or the export allowance.</li>
    <li>
      Decompiling or reverse engineering Obelisk, except where the law allows it (for example
      §§ 69d and 69e of the German Copyright Act).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Anyone who wants Obelisk can download the free trial from this site.</p>

  <h2>6. Passing your licence on</h2>
  <p>
    You may pass your licence on to someone else, as a whole. Tell us first: we issue a new
    licence file in their name, on the terms of your licence, and your own file stops working
    with versions released after that. You then stop using Obelisk under the licence and
    delete your copies of the licence file.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Your work</h2>
  <p>
    Your scans, exports, profiles and grading belong to you. Obelisk works on your computer and
    does not upload your images. If your trial or licence ends, your files, your exports and
    the grading saved beside your scans stay where they are.
  </p>

  <h2>8. If these terms are broken</h2>
  <p>
    If you seriously break these terms, for example by publishing your licence file, we may end
    your licence. Versions of Obelisk released after that will refuse it.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Warranty, liability and withdrawal</h2>
  <p>
    These follow the law and the terms and withdrawal information of our shop. Nothing in these
    licence terms limits your rights as a consumer.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Changes to these terms</h2>
  <p>
    Your licence keeps the terms that applied on the day you bought it. If we change these
    terms, the new version applies only to licences bought after the change. Every version
    stays listed on this page.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Version 1, 10 November 2026: this version.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>11. Contact</h2>
  <address>
    {L.name}{L.trading && <>, {L.trading}</>}<br />
    {L.street}, {L.city}, {L.country}<br />
    <a href={`mailto:${L.email}`}>{L.email}</a>
  </address>
</LegalPage>
